<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="eBPF:LE:64:default">
<!--
    Kuna #1652: P1 loader, eBPF ELF objects (no option: a correctness fix).
    The script loads a clang `-target bpf -O2 -c` object (source beside the
    fixture) and lets the loader pick the language from EM_BPF (247); the
    big-endian twin takes eBPF:BE:64:default the same way.

    Asserts #1-#2 prove the R_BPF_64_64 map reference lands in the two imm
    halves of the 16-byte lddw and the opcode survives: the function body is
    recovered and the map symbol is the lookup's first argument. Assert #3
    proves the `call 1` helper id takes the pspec default-symbol name.
    Assert #4 proves the R_BPF_64_32 subprogram call resolves to the callee
    in `.text` (`(S - P) / 8 - 1` into imm, not a 32-bit write over the
    opcode). Assert #5 proves the big-endian object loads and relocates too.
    Asserts #6-#7 load an object whose two static callees in `.text` are
    both named by the `.text` section symbol: the call's own `imm` (`-1`,
    then `2`) is the REL addend `(imm + 1) * 8` that selects the callee, so
    `prog` calls `twice` and then `plus7`, not `twice` twice; the
    big-endian twin keeps the source register in the low nibble of the
    register byte, and its calls are relocated too.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">9500000000000000</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
